title: A controls list
intro: A Controls List makes a game's controls screen by itself: a row for each input action, with a button that rebinds it showing every key it has.

project: Empty | Controls List

chapter: An action of the game's own | Drop Tool on Esc and Right Click
menu: Game > Controls...
controls
click: controls:addaction
actionname: DropTool
bind: Escape
expectcontrol: DropTool = Escape
bind: Mouse Right
say: Drop Tool is an action of the game's own, on Esc and on the right mouse button.
click: controls:close

chapter: A controls screen | Controls List on a panel
create: Panel
rename: Options
set: Panel.Open = true
add: ControlsList
explain: field:ControlsList.Actions | Actions | A Controls List makes a row for each action it lists. Here it's Drop Tool, then every action starting with Pan: Griff's own camera keys.
set: ControlsList.Actions = DropTool, Pan*
explain: field:ControlsList.Hide | Hide | Hide leaves some out. Pan Down can stay as it is.
set: ControlsList.Hide = PanDown
say: Adding it brought a Layout, which lines the rows up on the panel. The Scene view shows sample rows where they'll go.

chapter: Play | Rebind Drop Tool to Q
play
wait: 1
expectvalue: Key.Text.Shown = Esc / Right Click
say: A row for each action, its name and every key it has: Esc and Right Click for Drop Tool.
tap: Down
tap: Enter
wait: 0.5
say: Clicking its button waits for a new key.
tap: Q
wait: 0.5
expectvalue: Key.Text.Shown = Q / Right Click
say: Q takes Esc's place, and Right Click stays.
stop

chapter: A first and a second key | Slots
select: Options
set: ControlsList.Slots = 2
say: With two slots, each row has a button for its first key and one for its second, rebound one at a time.
play
wait: 1
expectvalue: Key 2.Text.Shown = Right Click
say: Pan Up's second key is the up arrow, and Drop Tool's is the right mouse button.
stop
